[Music]  hi I'm Jennifer Evans Carrio president  of sugar spun marketing author of  Pinterest marketing an hour a day in the  social media faculty chair at market  motive thanks for joining me for part 4  and the wrap-up in our series on  Facebook marketing one of the biggest  problems with social media from the  perspective of the user is that there  are so many different sites out there  that it's absolutely ridiculous  no one can stay on top of all of them  managing your login at multiple  locations and even figuring out how they  all work together is darn near  impossible one of the more brilliant  moves that Facebook has had in its  history is the idea of Facebook Connect  basically what Facebook Connect lets you  do is use Facebook's API and the  knowledge that the greatest majority of  human beings that are online are using  Facebook and it allows them to use their  facebook login as their login for  whatever website interface or program  you set up so whereas before people  would go to Facebook go to a brand page  then potentially come back around to  Facebook to do the sharing it allows  people to go directly to a brand or  directly to a resource or a website and  well on that website interact with their  Facebook account so it basically cut to  step out of the equation which makes it  far more likely that is people engage  with their brand that engagement that  interaction will actually show up on  Facebook published to their streams and  reach their friends and family now the  basic way of how it works is fairly  simple  you're integrating Facebook's like  button or a variety of other Facebook  options right into the content on your  website so for example here we have a  story from Mashable and at the top it  says do you like the story and if you  click that like button it's going to pop  up a little option that you can either  just go with hey I liked this or you can  actually add some content to the mix you  know give it your own description and  post it to your Facebook page and when  you push that post a Facebook button  it's going to show up there right in  your Facebook stream it's going to  publish out for everyone that you're  connected with and again from  at base website that the user was  choosing to interact with they can very  quickly and easily publish things out to  their Facebook feed and get it into the  news stream the reality is there's quite  a few social plug in options using  Facebook Connect a couple of the more  popular ones the like button you'll see  this popping up all over the web you'll  see it on ecommerce sites on content  sites pretty much everywhere is that  ubiquitous little Facebook like buttons  that people click and as soon as they  click it it broadcasts out to their  network hey this is content that these  people actually like there's also the  send button where the share button  depending on how long ago it's been  implemented it can actually pop up  either way this one you'll see often  times showing up alongside content or  videos sometimes on e-commerce sites but  usually it's content and video focus and  this basically lets you take the content  that you see and send it to Facebook  send it to a group send it to someone  that you're connected with but basically  not just share it to your wall but  actually send it through to specific  people that you're connected with on  Facebook there's also a comments plug-in  that basically allows anyone that has a  blog or again even an e-commerce site to  integrate comments from Facebook right  there into their page so you can be  reading someone's blog post and at the  end of the blog post you're going to see  that add a comment button and it's going  to show you logged into your Facebook  account because your Facebook account  will basically follow you all over the  web unless you have your cookies turned  off so this gives you the opportunity to  have those comments taking place on your  site but to also have it publishing back  to Facebook because as that happens say  when Katie asked and makes a post using  her Facebook commenting it then  publishes out and her friend Rita  McCartin is going to see it on Facebook  and can click into the website read the  post and then once again leave a comment  that can turn around and publish out to  somebody else there's also the idea of  the Facebook Activity Feed this  basically shows up on your content site  it lets a person who comes to your site  see which of the people they're  connected with have liked this page and  sometimes it will even show  the people that they're connected with  that have liked a specific page shared a  specific page commented on a page  basically lets you see which friends  have been active on a site and what  they're doing and then finally the idea  of the registration integration and this  is what we talked about a couple slides  ago the idea that you can use your  facebook login to connect to a new  website and to basically run your  profile on that site so Pinterest one of  the many many examples of companies that  will just let you sign in with your  Facebook account without creating any  new registration information once you  connect with some of these sites using  your Facebook account the great thing  about it you then have access to your  friends from within Facebook so example  if you're on a site like Pinterest and  you're looking to connect with more  friends on the site it's a great way to  go through and say well which of my  facebook friends are also on Pinterest  so I can come through and follow them or  which of my facebook friends are also on  Instagram or on Twitter so that I can  connect with them and follow them now  the developers page at Facebook does  keep a running tab of all of the social  plugins that are available gives you a  great walkthrough of what they are how  they work and will also help you  generate the code that you need to plug  into your web page in order to integrate  these different options so you can read  to that at developers facebook.com slash  Doc's slash plugins now the  possibilities using Facebook Connect are  pretty expensive  think from the social gaming platform  you know think how big social gaming is  on Facebook or on mobile apps the fact  that you can connect it to a user's  Facebook account and publish updates to  their games and they've reached  such-and-such level or you know they  just scored how many points and words  with friends all of it gives you the  opportunity to tie interactivity with  your brand into the Facebook page of the  person that you're dealing with so that  again you get some of that social  sharing promotion and marketing effort  also the ability to have some friend  integration you know pulling in the  profile the pictures the contact  information so think of the example I  showed from Pinterest where you can  actually go in and see which of your  friends are also using Pinterest so that  you're able to connect with them voting  systems can come into play here you know  basically you can bring people in for  polls that say you know hey come and  share your opinion on this you  vote on this idea or this concept and  have it connect back to their Facebook  account so that they can then encourage  their friends and family to come in and  vote you know or have have their say on  a specific topic and then the same thing  with forums it gives people the  opportunity to connect with their  Facebook friends because they may not  have realized that people they were  friends with on Facebook we're also  present on this forum or this website or  in this discussion area so having that  tie-in helps people see those social  connections taking place all over the  web and really maximize the ability to  interact with the people that they care  about now another aspect of Facebook  that needs to be considered is the idea  of check-ins you know a few years back  check-ins were starting to run really  well on things like koala and Foursquare  but his Facebook introduced Facebook  places and started encouraging people to  check in as part of their status updates  we definitely saw some changes in that 1  in 5 smartphone users checks in each  month using some type of location-based  service Facebook continues to steal  market share from Foursquare and Gowalla  because again there's no need to go use  another service if you're already using  Facebook regularly anyways now the  demographics on smartphones sharing and  the social check-ins that tends to be  people who are in the younger age group  so between 25 and 34 mostly full-time  workers people who are out of college  and professionally and gainfully  employed these are the people that tend  to utilize that activity the most so if  you have a brick and mortar store if you  are doing some type of event if this is  your target market you want to make sure  that you're doing something to help  motivate those check-ins motivate that  sharing so that it will broadcast out to  the rest of their network of course in  order to check into a place you actually  have to have a place listing on Facebook  now for the most part Facebook will  actually go through sort of the yellow  page listings you know or the Google  Places listing and they'll pull business  names and addresses and phone numbers  and general basic information and  they'll do their best based on  geolocation to actually pop that up as  an option for people when they check in  what will work much better for you is if  you actually take that Places listing  and you combine it with the Facebook  page for your brick-and-mortar business  so if you go and you run a search for  your company name and you see if you can  locate it if it is still showing up with  just the generalized little location dot  and it's not your Facebook business page  then you need to look over in the Edit  area for the option to claimant B do you  know the owner is this your business you  create a page this gives you the chance  to take that existing listing and either  turn it into a full-fledged page or  merge it with the existing page that you  have so if you check that hole is this  your business they're going to basically  take you through a verification process  it's going to require phone or document  verification just prove you are who you  say you are then they'll give you the  ability to merge it in with the official  Facebook page that you've created for  this business so that way when people  check in and their friends see it if  they happen to click on say the Carleton  Motel will actually take them to the  Facebook page for the Carlton Hotel  rather than just the standard listing  now this can also work really well for  events because if you're promoting  events you're setting them up on  Facebook you're inviting people in to  RSVP for them if they have RSVP'd for an  event they can also then check in to  that event when they actually show up at  it because it will have the address the  location everything will be tied back in  so it's a great way to encourage your  followers to connect with you you know a  lot of e-commerce companies and a lot of  brick-and-mortar stores are using it for  special events or for you know previews  of new items and clothing that are  coming out but they're even also using  it just for sales to say hey here's this  big sale that we have going on here's  what we're going to have for offer you  can discuss what your strategy is  men and jobbing you can post pictures  about what's going to be going on it's  basically just another way to get the  buzz in the conversation going on  Facebook about what's going on in your  store now when it comes to Facebook a  lot of companies have a hard time trying  to figure out okay how do we get rolling  you know what's our priority need to be  do we just get in and start posting do  we need to spend a bunch of time  observing how does this work so a good  rule of thumb is when you start you had  to build a page because if you don't  have a page nobody's going to show up  nobody has a way to connect with you so  even if you don't put a ton of content  on it yet get some type of basic level  presence up and running look at what  your cover photo is look at what your  content is going to be and then start to  create a Content plan you know figure  out what do you want to put out there  what do you think is going to be a good  fit for your target market that's when  you want to start soliciting followers  so you may decide to do that through  some advertising and some paid and  sponsored posts you may decide to do it  via some of your other channels maybe  your blog or your Twitter feed maybe you  want to put fliers in the bag of every  person who comes in and shops at your  store but basically you have to start  soliciting some followers then you want  to start offering content to those  followers and you want to start looking  at which pieces of content they're  interacting with you know how is your  EdgeRank doing how much exposure and how  much virality are you getting from the  content you're putting out there and  then interact with them you know don't  just put up the content let them respond  and do nothing if people are responding  to you respond back and engage them in  conversation thank you so much for  joining me for this series on Facebook  marketing  hey once you become an expert in digital  marketing then subscribe to the simple  learned Channel and click here to watch  more such videos turn up and get  certified in digital marketing click  here

